                <pb facs="0355" n="285" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg285"/>

                <head>No. 146.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Morton to Mr. Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-11-10"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 10, 1883</date>.
                        (Received December 1.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 441.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your telegram of September 26,
                    authorizing Mr. Vignaud and myself to attend the conference for the protection
                    of submarine cables, and also to your No. 359 of October 1, inclosing our formal
                    powers to negotiate, conclude and sign the same subject to the ratification of
                    the President of the United States, by and with the advice and consent of the
                    Senate, I have the honor to send herewith a joint report of Mr. Vignaud and
                    myself reporting our action in the conference, and transmitting a copy of the
                    convention as signed on the 26th of October.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>LEYI P. MORTON.</signed></closer>





